/-!
# Field selection for linear alpha and binary or dihedral beta

Two distinct beta centers are disjoint subgroups of the alpha center.
The literal seed counts bound |Z_beta| by 2q_alpha. At equality the two
centers become complementary Sylow fixed spaces of the actual alpha-center
representation. Natural-module recognition would force the alpha field
exponent to be one, contradicting the small-alpha exclusion. Powers of two
therefore give |Z_beta| <= q_alpha.

The resulting module has dimension at most 2n+1, has no invariant vectors,
and is generated by its Sylow fixed space. The small-degree lemma makes it
irreducible. Its Schur degree is both the Sylow-fixed dimension and a divisor
of n and dim V. The seed count gives dim V=1+n+s, so s=1. The minimum-degree
bound now forces n=2 and |Z_beta|=2. This proves the field relation for every
linear-alpha/binary-beta marking, and gives the same selection in the
D10-beta candidate before its actual extraspecial obstruction is applied.

No field-four, center-order, irreducibility, or module-coordinate conclusion
is assumed. The core quotients and conjugation action are the original ones.
Other beta rows and the general alpha-row selection remain further source
obligations. Source: Delgado--Goldschmidt--Stellmacher, Groups and Graphs
(1985), (11.6)--(11.7), printed pp.172--175.
-/

public noncomputable section
open Subgroup
open scoped IsMulCommutative MatrixGroups
namespace CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterF.Source.DGS85.CommutingOneProfile
